    stem: "In this edited extract the historian Mary E. Daly comments on the life of women in the 1960s. The 1960s gave women greater opportunities to work as secretaries, clerks and teachers. Women also had better marriage prospects than in the past, with a hope of settling down in a house with modern amenities. There is good reason to believe that many women were content to become full-time wives and mothers, not least because so many of their predecessors had been denied this lifestyle. While more girls than boys attended secondary school, girls were more likely to leave at fifteen-sixteen and less likely to attend university. Paid employment was a temporary phase between school and marriage for most women.",
