    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, History 2024 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "hist-2024-ol-lm-s2t3a-q1", topicId: "hist-lm-irl-3", conceptId: "building-connolly-located-during-easter-week",
    section: "2", questionRef: "2024 OL Later Modern Section 2 Topic 3 A Q1",
    stem: "This edited extract refers to the execution of one of the leaders of the 1916 Rising. Read it and answer the questions which follow. Because of the serious injuries he suffered in the GPO during Easter Week, James Connolly was imprisoned in a small ward in the hospital wing of Dublin Castle. It was there that he was court-marshalled and condemned to death on 9 May. Later that day he was visited by his wife, Lillie, and his daughter, Nora. They were hopeful that, because of his injuries, he might not be executed. However, on the following day Connolly was informed that he would be executed at dawn on 11 May. ‘Execution suspended by Prime Minister’s order’ is written on Connolly’s file. This order was ignored by General Sir John Maxwell. At dawn on 11 May Connolly was brought by ambulance to Kilmainham Gaol. Because he was unable to stand, he was strapped in a chair and executed by firing squad.",
    questionText: "In what building was Connolly located during Easter Week?",
